  { question: "What should I do immediately after an accident in Jackson County?", answer: "First, ensure everyone's safety and call 911 if there are injuries. If you're able, document the scene with photos, exchange information with other parties involved, and obtain contact information from witnesses. Report the accident to police and notify your own insurance company within one year to preserve your PIP benefits. Avoid admitting fault or providing recorded statements to other insurers without consulting an attorney. Contact us as soon as possible for a free consultation to protect your rights." },
  { question: "How does Michigan's no-fault insurance system work for Jackson County accident victims?", answer: "Under Michigan's no-fault system, you file a claim with your own auto insurance for Personal Injury Protection (PIP) benefits—regardless of who caused the accident. PIP covers medical expenses, lost wages (up to 85% of gross income), and replacement services. Your coverage level depends on your policy choice: unlimited, $500,000, $250,000, or $50,000. Understanding your coverage before an accident is critical. We help Jackson County residents navigate this complex system and secure all benefits they're entitled to receive." },
  { question: "Can I sue the at-fault driver after a Jackson County car accident?", answer: "Yes, but only if your injuries meet Michigan's serious impairment of body function threshold—an objectively manifested impairment of an important body function that affects your general ability to lead your normal life. If you meet this threshold, you can pursue compensation for pain and suffering, excess medical expenses, and damages beyond your PIP coverage. Our attorneys have extensive experience proving threshold injuries and handling third-party claims for Jackson County clients." },
  { question: "What is the statute of limitations for personal injury cases in Jackson County?", answer: "In Michigan, the statute of limitations for most personal injury cases is three years from the date of the injury. For wrongful death claims, the three-year period runs from the date of death. No-fault insurance benefits must be claimed within one year of the accident. However, specific circumstances can affect these deadlines. Consulting an attorney promptly ensures you preserve all potential claims and don't lose your right to compensation." },
  { question: "How much does it cost to hire a Jackson County personal injury attorney?", answer: "At Logeman & Iafrate, P.C., we handle personal injury cases on a contingency-fee basis. That means you pay nothing up front, and we don't charge legal fees unless we recover compensation for you. We advance all case costs, including expert witness fees and court expenses. You can afford experienced, trial-ready attorneys from day one—with no upfront cost. Schedule a free consultation to discuss your case and learn how we can help." },
  { question: "What makes your firm different from other Jackson County personal injury lawyers?", answer: "We take cases other firms turn down and prepare every case for trial. With more than 45 years of trial experience behind us, insurance companies know we are ready to try a case. That trial-ready approach has resulted in decades of verdicts and settlements throughout Michigan. We're not a high-volume settlement mill. We're experienced trial lawyers who fight by your side and take your case to a jury when that's what it takes to secure the compensation you deserve." },
];
